# Wait alerts and install

An alert is "tell me when this lane at this crossing drops under N minutes." It is not part of `preference`. `wait_alert` is one row per `(user_id, port_number, mode)` with `threshold` (5 to 180, whole minutes) and `below`, the server's memory of the last check. `push_subscription` is one row per device, keyed by the push `endpoint`. Both tables cascade from `profile` and are created on first use by `ensureTables()`. An account holds at most `MAX_ALERTS`.

`lib/alert.ts` is client-safe. Do not import server modules into it. `parsePushDevice` only accepts `https` endpoints on the real push services (Google, Mozilla, Apple, Windows), because the server posts to that address.

## The check

`GET /api/cron/alerts` runs once a minute from `vercel.json`. It requires `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`, and a missing `CRON_SECRET` rejects everything. `runAlertCheck()` reads `getBorderWaitTimes()` (the one cached CBP fetch, so the check can lag the feed by its cache window), then for each alert uses `readLane`:

- `minutes < threshold` and `below` is false: `claimBelow` flips it in one `update ... where below = false`, so overlapping runs notify once. Then it pushes to every device of that account. If no device accepted, `below` goes back to false and the next minute retries.
- `below` is true and `minutes >= threshold + REARM_SLACK`: `below` goes false, so the next drop notifies again.
- `minutes` is null: a closed lane (`tone === "closed"`) re-arms. A pending update changes nothing.

A new alert saves `below` from the current reading, so setting one on an already-short lane waits for the next drop. A push that answers 404 or 410 deletes that device. The payload is written in the owner's `preference.locale` (English when unset), has a 10 minute TTL, and its `tag` is `port:mode` so a newer alert replaces the old one. It links to `crossingSharePath`.

## Schedules

A schedule is "at this time, on these days, send me the current wait at these bridges." It is separate from `wait_alert` and `preference`. `schedule` is one row per schedule (`id`, `minute` after local midnight in 5 minute steps, `days` as a bitmask with Sunday as bit 0, `time_zone`, one `mode` for the whole schedule, `ports` as jsonb, `name` or null, `enabled`, `last_sent_on` as a local `YYYY-MM-DD`). `name` is optional, at most `MAX_SCHEDULE_NAME` (40) characters; a blank name is stored as null. It cascades from `profile` and is created on first use. An account holds at most `MAX_SCHEDULES` (8), each with at most `MAX_SCHEDULE_PORTS` (6) bridges. `lib/schedule.ts` is client-safe; `schedule.server.ts` and `schedule-check.server.ts` are not.

The same cron route runs `runScheduleCheck()` after the alert check; one failing does not stop the other. A schedule is due when, on the wall clock of its own time zone, today is one of its days, the time is within `SEND_WINDOW` (5) minutes after its time, and `last_sent_on` is not today. Comparing wall-clock time, not a stored UTC hour, is what keeps it right across daylight saving. `claimSend` sets `last_sent_on` in one statement so overlapping runs send once. If no device accepted, `releaseSend` clears it and the next minute in the window retries. If none of its bridges are in the feed, the day stays claimed and nothing is sent. Saving a schedule whose time has already passed today marks today as sent, so it starts tomorrow.

The push is one notification, in the owner's locale, tagged `schedule:id:date`. With a name, the title is that name and the first body line is `time · lane`. Without one, the title is `time · lane`. Then one body line per bridge (`readLane`, with the status when there is no minute count). A tap opens `/`. The time zone is the device's, sent on every save. `AlertSync` re-registers the device for an account with alerts or schedules. Saving asks for notification permission from the tap, like a wait alert.

The screen is the third tab, `/schedules`: `SchedulesPane` (cards with a pause switch) and `ScheduleSheet`. Creating or editing walks bridge, then time, then days. An optional name sits above those steps; it shows on the card and becomes the notification title. The bridge step lists the wallet (a new schedule starts with none chosen) and the lane sits there with it. At `lg` it takes the right column from Browse, and the account menu switches between the two. `parseAppPath` checks `/schedules` before the port rule because the word is shaped like a port number.

## The device

`public/sw.js` shows pushes and handles taps. It caches nothing and does not touch fetches. `next.config.ts` serves it with `Cache-Control: no-cache`. A tap focuses an open window or opens the card, same origin only.

The clock button on `CardFace`, just left of share, opens the picker for the lane on screen: off, or a threshold. It shows the chosen minutes once an alert is on. That press does not open, reorder, or swipe the card. There is no test button in the app.

`POST /api/alerts/test` sends one test to the device named in the body and changes no alert. Each test uses a new tag. Do not set `requireInteraction`: on macOS that skips the banner and only fills Notification Center. Do not also show the alert inside the page. The system notification is the only one.

`enablePush()` runs from the tap on a threshold chip. It asks permission first, with nothing awaited before it, then registers the worker, subscribes with `NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`, and `PUT`s `/api/push-subscription`. A device belongs to whoever registered it last. Sign-out calls `disablePush()` while the session still exists, which deletes the device row and unsubscribes. `AlertSync` re-registers the device on open when the account has alerts and permission is already granted. It never prompts.

iPhone Safari has no `PushManager` until the site runs from the Home Screen, so `pushSupport()` reports `needs-install` there and the alert control points at the install steps instead of prompting. The Home Screen copy has its own storage, so the person signs in again inside it.

Env: `NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`, `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY`, `VAPID_SUBJECT`, `CRON_SECRET`. Generate the pair with `web-push generate-vapid-keys`. Changing the public key invalidates every existing subscription. Per-minute cron needs Vercel Pro; Hobby allows once a day. Anywhere else, any scheduler that sends the bearer token works.

## Install

`installStore` (client-only; its server snapshot offers nothing) holds Chrome's `beforeinstallprompt` event until our button uses it, and reports `standalone`, `ios`, and `offerable`. Safari has no prompt, so `start()` opens `InstallSheet` with steps. `InstallBanner` sits under the wallet header below `lg`, hides while a card is open, and "Not now" holds it back 14 days (`usbordertime.install.dismissed.v1`). The account menu entry stays available while `offerable`. Nothing shows once the app runs from the Home Screen.

All copy is `COPY` in `lib/i18n.ts`, in both languages. The privacy page names alerts and device addresses; keep it true when this changes.
